Categories derived from the origin’s causal prompt: From the origin, the system identifies major categories that represent distinct causal factors or subproblems implied by the prompt. Each category is determined by analyzing the origin prompt for key themes or drivers of the issue. These categories form the first level of subdivision of the problem, much like the primary branches in a cause-and-effect diagram (Ishikawa/Fishbone) which organize causes into major classes​
ASQ.ORG
. The categories are chosen declaratively based on the content of the origin: for example, if the origin prompt is a complex question, the system parses it and assigns parts of the question to different categories of inquiry. This ensures that the set of categories collectively covers the entire problem space established by the origin’s metadata.
Assigning weights in a hierarchical T-structure: The origin distributes weights to each category, indicating their relative importance or contribution to the overall problem. Likewise, each category assigns weights to its subcategories. This creates a two-level hierarchy of influence that can be visualized as a "T-structure": the top of the T (the origin) fans out to the categories, and each category in turn fans out (downwards) to its subcategories. All these connections carry weights that are explicitly defined in the metadata. For instance, if the origin prompt suggests Category A is twice as critical as Category B, the weight from origin to A will be higher than to B. Similarly, within Category A, if Subcategory A1 addresses a more significant cause than A2, A1 gets a higher weight. This weighting scheme is declarative: at any time one can inspect the metadata and see the influence values from the origin to each category and from each category to its sub-nodes. The hierarchy ensures traceability of influence – the sum of subcategory weights rolls up to the category weight, and category weights tie back to the origin. (In the fishbone analogy, this parallels how sub-causes branch off main causes, indicating their share in the effect​
