, though here we quantify that share.) The T-structure thus provides a clear, weighted map of the problem from general (origin) to specific (subcategories).
Causal relevance across categories (off-diagonal interactions): In addition to the tree of direct weights, the framework accounts for cross-category relevance. This means the system can capture causal links between elements that are not in a direct parent-child relationship. Such links appear as off-diagonal entries in an influence matrix (where diagonal entries represent the direct hierarchical links). For example, a subcategory under Category X might also impact Category Y; the pipeline would represent this with a link from that subcategory (or Category X) to Category Y, with its own weight. These cross-category interactions are computed based on the origin metadata and domain knowledge – the system declaratively specifies a link if a cause in one category clearly affects another category. In well-structured metadata, a cause may even be listed under multiple categories to reflect this overlap​
