2. Cross-Category Influence and Dynamic Sorting
Causal links beyond direct adjacency: The framework explicitly allows and defines causal links that go beyond parent-child adjacency in the hierarchy. In practical terms, this means the cause-effect network isn’t limited to the tree structure (origin → category → subcategory); any node can potentially influence any other, if causality warrants it. The rules for these links are declarative: a cross-link is established if there is a justified causal relationship or dependency. For example, if Category X deals with "economic factors" and Category Y with "social factors," and a specific economic sub-factor directly causes a social outcome, the system will create a link from that sub-factor in X to the relevant part of Y. These rules ensure such a link carries a metadata entry describing its origin, target, and nature of influence (possibly with a weight as described above). The existence of a link does not restructure the hierarchy – it overlays on top of the T-structure. All nodes remain in their primary category, but now with references pointing to others as needed. This approach yields a directed acyclic graph of causes where the tree is the backbone and cross-links are lateral connections. By design, every such connection must be validated by the pipeline’s logic (or user input) as a real causal relation, preventing random or spurious links. The ability to form non-adjacent links means the system can capture complex cause-effect chains faithfully (e.g., a cause in Category A leading to an effect in Category B). Notably, when one cause is relevant in multiple categories, the framework might represent it in each relevant place​
ASQ.ORG
 with links tying those representations together, rather than duplicating the data arbitrarily. This preserves a single conceptual cause with multiple impacts. All these cross-category link rules are part of the framework’s declarative knowledge – they can be inspected or queried at any time to understand why two elements are linked.
Expected cross-category interactions in well-structured subdivisions: The framework assumes that if the problem has been divided into proper categories, then truly significant causal interactions will often be across those categories rather than within one category. This is because each category is meant to encapsulate a distinct aspect of the problem. Interactions within a single category tend to be more straightforward (handled by the subcategory breakdown), whereas the more interesting, emergent relationships happen between different aspects. In a well-structured decomposition, categories are relatively independent facets of the problem, and any dependency or influence between them is notable. Therefore, the presence of cross-category causal links is expected and desired – it indicates that the categories are not redundant subdivisions of the same thing, but complementary pieces of a larger puzzle that affect one another. For instance, a “Technology” category and a “Market” category in a business analysis might be separate, but technological changes could strongly influence market behavior; a cross-link captures this. If our categories are well-chosen, such a link will exist (because technology and market are indeed distinct domains that interact). Conversely, if we found all interactions happening within a single category, it could mean the initial split wasn’t orthogonal and might need revision. Thus the framework treats cross-category links as a sign of a healthy model of the problem. It ensures these interactions are recorded (no cross-impact is left unmodeled) and uses them to inform the holistic solution. The idea is corroborated by cause-analysis best practices, where the same underlying cause might appear under multiple major categories​
ASQ.ORG
 – indicating that the categories cover different angles of the issue and the cause in question bridges them. By expecting and accounting for cross-category influences, the pipeline can handle complex scenarios where different domains of the problem space collide or cooperate in producing outcomes.
Dynamic sorting triggered by edits (matrix re-ordering): The FIMPipeline maintains an ordered structure (often conceptualized as a matrix or list) of categories and subcategories according to certain criteria (such as priority, weight, or logical sequence). There are rules in place that define how this ordering should appear (for example, categories might be sorted by descending weight, or by a predefined logical order, etc., and similarly within each category). The framework is dynamic, meaning any change to the data or metadata can cause a re-evaluation of these order rules. If a user edits a weight, adds a new subcategory, changes a link, or if the system itself refines something that affects order, the pipeline will automatically check if the current ordering still respects all rules. When an inconsistency or “check failure” is detected – say a category’s weight is now out of descending order – the system automatically re-sorts the affected part of the structure. This is done immediately and declaratively: the framework’s logic reorders the elements according to the declared sorting rules, without needing external commands. Importantly, the re-sorting is as localized and minimal as possible. Only the elements that need repositioning will be moved, while unaffected portions remain in place​
