. For example, if one subcategory’s weight increases and it needs to rise in its parent category’s list, the system will insert it into the correct position among its siblings and shift others accordingly, but everything else stays put. This dynamic reordering can be thought of like maintaining a sorted list where insertions or updates keep the list sorted at all times. The “matrix” of category-to-category relevance is similarly subject to reordering – if cross-category link strengths are used to order categories, then changes there trigger resorting too. Every edit triggers a cascade of validate → sort if needed operations, which the pipeline handles autonomously. This guarantees that at any given moment, the structural order the user or subsequent computations see is consistent with the latest data. The framework’s declarative nature means the desired state (properly sorted list/matrix) is always defined, and the system continuously drives itself towards that state whenever changes occur. The end result is that the hierarchy and any tables derived from it are always up-to-date and correctly ordered, without manual review. (For instance, if Category C was listed after Category B but an edit makes C more important, the system might swap their positions and update all relevant indices instantly.) By dynamically sorting and re-sorting, the FIMPipeline upholds its structured order rules in real-time.
3. Development Milestones and Preconditions for Higher Dimensions
Conditions before expanding beyond 1D ordering: The current framework organizes information in essentially one dimension – a single ordered hierarchy (with the hierarchy often linearized for sorting). Before this is extended into higher dimensions (such as a 2D grid of factors, or adding additional axes like time or scenarios), certain conditions must be met. The one-dimensional (1D) ordering must be fully stable and validated. This means that all the rules governing the 1D structure (categorization, weighting, linking, sorting, refinement, etc.) work reliably, and the system can maintain consistency within this simpler structure. Key milestones include: no unresolved sorting issues remain in 1D, the pipeline can handle the addition/removal/refinement of categories without error, and the full cycle of dynamic updating (as described above) operates smoothly. Essentially, the 1D framework should be self-consistent and capable of handling the complexity of the problem space on its single axis. Only after achieving this will the pipeline consider adding more complexity via additional dimensions. The rationale is to avoid compounding uncertainties – if the system struggles in one dimension, adding another would exacerbate the problems. Therefore, a formal checkpoint is established: do not move to higher dimensions until the 1D system meets all correctness and performance benchmarks. This includes stress-testing the 1D system with large numbers of categories and cross-links to ensure it behaves predictably. Once these conditions are satisfied, the groundwork is laid to introduce a second dimension (for example, layering another categorization scheme perpendicular to the first, or mapping the 1D structure across time slices, etc.). The expansion itself will come with its own new rules, but it hinges on the foundation being rock-solid. In summary, the pipeline’s evolution is gated by milestones that ensure the current dimensional complexity is mastered before additional complexity is introduced.
Self-legending and proprioception as preconditions for multi-dimensional expansion: Before the framework can safely expand into multiple dimensions, it must have robust self-legending and proprioceptive processing in the 1D version. Self-legending means the system can generate and update a clear description or “legend” of its own structure. In practice, the pipeline should be able to output an organized summary of the hierarchy: listing categories, subcategories, their IDs, weights, and relationships in a human-readable form (like an automatically generated documentation or legend for the matrix). This capability assures that even as complexity grows, the system’s state remains interpretable – anyone (or any debugging process) can query the pipeline and get an accurate map of the current model. Proprioceptive processing refers to the system’s internal self-awareness – it knows its own configuration and can detect changes or anomalies. In the 1D framework, this translates to the pipeline being able to track how many categories it has, which ones are active, what links exist, etc., as internal knowledge. It should sense, for example, if a category is out of order or if a weight sum doesn’t normalize, without external checks. This concept is analogous to an organism’s proprioception (sensing positions of its body parts); here the pipeline “senses” its components and their status​
