. The declarative rules for self-monitoring must be fully implemented: the system should continuously maintain metadata about its own structure (like a meta-matrix of its state). Achieving self-legending and proprioception in 1D is critical because in higher dimensions the complexity increases dramatically – without these, the system could become opaque and uncontrollable. As a concrete precondition, the pipeline must demonstrate, in the 1D environment, that it can update its self-legend whenever a change occurs and catch any internal inconsistencies via proprioceptive checks. This includes testing that the legend always matches the actual structure and that the system flags any deviations (and possibly auto-corrects them as discussed). Only when the pipeline can reliably explain and regulate itself in one dimension do we allow it to grow. This approach follows the principle of introspection before extension: a system should not become more complex until it can understand and manage its current complexity. (This idea is mirrored in other systems – for example, CORBA systems carry self-describing metadata so components can discover and check each other at runtime​
