.) In summary, verifying self-legending and proprioception in 1D is a mandatory milestone before multi-dimensional expansion.
Dynamic sorting with prefix reassignment (order integrity without ID change): One specific rule to enforce, especially as the system grows, is that reordering operations must not invalidate references. Every category and subcategory in the pipeline has a unique identifier (ID) that is permanent. The prefix or position label of an item (like “1.” for the first category, “1.3” for a third sub-item of category 1, etc.) is assigned based on the current sorted order and can change when the order changes, but the underlying ID of the item never changes. The framework declaratively guarantees this separation of identity and order. When a dynamic re-sorting occurs (whether in 1D or future multi-dimensional arrangements), the system will recalculate the prefixes of items to reflect the new ordering without altering their IDs or their internal link references. For example, suppose Category X has ID 5 and is labeled "3. X" (meaning third in order). If X moves to first position, it will be relabeled "1. X", but it remains ID 5 internally; all metadata that pointed to ID 5 still correctly points to Category X. This rule is crucial for maintaining order integrity. It means that any pointers, causal links, or external references (like a user’s notes referring to a category ID) remain valid after reordering. The dynamic sorting mechanism thus includes a step of updating all human-facing ordering labels (and any order-dependent data) while preserving the core data structure. The pipeline may maintain a mapping of order → ID and ID → order that it updates on each sort. By doing so, it ensures consistency: the sorted view is always correct, and the identities remain stable. In a multi-dimensional expansion, this principle extends similarly — e.g., an item might have coordinates (like row 2, column 1 in a 2D matrix) that can change if things shuffle, but its ID remains constant. The framework will dynamically check this rule as part of its integrity validations, triggering automatic fixes if, say, an ID got accidentally duplicated or a label didn’t update. Ultimately, this guarantees that the content of the pipeline can be re-ordered or re-arranged freely in response to edits or new dimensions without ever breaking the references that glue the system together.
All rules dynamically checked with automatic resolution cycles: The FIMPipeline framework is governed by a comprehensive set of declarative rules (such as those about weighting, ordering, linking, self-description, etc.). These rules are not only documented but also actively enforced by the system at runtime. The pipeline includes a continuous monitoring loop (or event-driven checks) that dynamically checks each rule condition. For instance, it will check that all category weights from the origin sum to 1 (if that’s a rule), or that no category is orphaned without an origin link, or that the prefixes are in sequential order, and so on – covering every rule defined in the framework. If at any point a rule is found to be violated, the system immediately initiates an automatic resolution cycle. In this cycle, the pipeline attempts to resolve the inconsistency using predefined strategies. This could mean recalculating something (e.g., normalizing weights again), resorting the structure (as described), reassigning a link, or invoking a particular fix routine associated with the rule. The process is additive and iterative – the system keeps applying resolutions and then re-checking all rules, in a loop, until all rules are satisfied. Only when the framework’s state passes all checks does the cycle conclude. All of this happens under the hood, ensuring the user always sees a consistent state or, at worst, a transient state that is quickly corrected. Importantly, these resolution cycles are automatic – no manual intervention is needed or allowed for maintaining rule integrity, which removes human error from the enforcement process. The declarative framework essentially “self-heals” to comply with its declarative constraints. This dynamic enforcement covers existing rules and is extensible to new rules that might be added when the system scales to higher dimensions (the rule-checker and resolver will simply get more conditions to verify). In sum, the pipeline behaves like a live system that’s constantly validating its structure against the framework’s specification and taking corrective action whenever something diverges. This guarantees that even as complexity increases, the system will not drift into an invalid state unnoticed – it will catch and fix issues proactively. The end result is high reliability: the structure you see always adheres to the declared framework rules, achieved through ongoing dynamic checking and resolution.
