Parallelization principles (conceptual and hardware): The FIMPipeline is designed from the ground up with parallel execution in mind, both in theory and in practice. At a conceptual level, the framework isolates independent tasks so they can proceed concurrently. For example, processing each top-level category can be considered a separate thread of work – since categories are largely independent in the first pass, the heavy lifting (like analyzing subcategories, gathering data for each category) can happen in parallel. The declarative structure helps this: because relationships and dependencies are explicitly declared, the system knows which parts must be synchronized and which can run freely. The goal is to maximize the portion of the pipeline that can be parallelized and minimize the sequential dependencies, recognizing that overall speed-up is limited by any sequential bottlenecks (per Amdahl’s Law, the performance gain is capped by the fraction of the process that is sequential​
