.)
Single-machine initial deployment, with future distributed scalability in mind: At present, the FIMPipeline is implemented on a single machine, which simplifies development and debugging. All components (the origin processor, category handlers, etc.) run in one process or a set of processes on one computer, using shared memory for fast communication. However, critically, the architecture is engineered such that moving to a distributed (multi-machine) environment in the future will require minimal changes to the core logic. The declarative framework aids this by cleanly separating concerns and interactions. For example, each category’s processing can be encapsulated in a module that communicates with others via well-defined messages or data packets (which currently may be function calls or in-memory data transfers, but could be converted to network messages). The pipeline avoids assumptions like “all memory is accessible at once” – instead it treats data exchange as passing of information, which could just as well happen over a network. Because of this modular design, one could deploy, say, half the categories on one server and half on another, and as long as they send their results to a coordinator (or share partial results for cross-links), the overall system still behaves the same. In preparation for that, the developers have ensured there is a clear abstraction layer for communication between parts of the pipeline (even if it’s just function calls today, it’s structured so that it could be replaced with RPC calls tomorrow). Parallelization on a single machine (multithreading) is often the first step, and that is already in place as discussed. The next step would be multiprocessing (on the same machine or across machines), which the system can accommodate by running each major component in its own process and using an IPC (inter-process communication) mechanism – again facilitated by the declarative separation of “what needs to be communicated” from “how it’s communicated”. The framework also keeps in mind data consistency in a distributed scenario: for instance, the rule-checking and resolution cycle might run in a master node that gathers reports from all worker nodes. Nothing in the core algorithm assumes a single memory space, so there is no fundamental barrier to distribution. In summary, while the current mode is single-machine, all the groundwork (modularity, message-based coordination, parallel-safe data structures) has been laid to scale out to a cluster. The system can thus evolve from a prototype into an enterprise or cloud deployment without a redesign, simply by allocating components to different machines and establishing the network communication between them. This ensures the longevity of the framework – it’s not limited by the hardware configuration and can grow to meet future demands while adhering to the same declarative principles.